---
/**
 * Quantum sensing. The page follows the home page's form: a hero with one figure, then sections
 * that each open with a question, state their point as the heading, and show one figure. The
 * story and every sentence come from `openspec/notes/quantum-site/04_sensing_page_draft.md`, which
 * traces each fact to `03_sensing_raw_information.md`.
 *
 * Numbers read from `src/data/sensing.ts`; output lines are copied from runs.
 */
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import { rustDark, rustLight } from '../../../shiki-rust-themes.mjs';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/nav/SiteHeader.astro';
import HomeSection from '../../components/home/HomeSection.astro';
import TrackFigure from '../../components/sensing/TrackFigure.astro';
import SystematicsMatrix from '../../components/sensing/SystematicsMatrix.astro';
import CampaignBars from '../../components/sensing/CampaignBars.astro';
import EdgeCards from '../../components/sensing/EdgeCards.astro';
import {
  FIELD,
  GRADIOMETER,
  GRAVIMETER,
  VERIFICATION,
  gradiometerCandidates,
  gradiometerExperiments,
  verificationCommand,
  signed,
  systematics,
  verifications,
} from '../../data/sensing';
import { CRATE_URL } from '../../consts';
import { numberWord } from '../../data/evidence';

const SHIKI = { light: rustLight, dark: rustDark };
const title = 'Quantum sensing | DeepCausality Quantum';
const description =
  'Discover the cause of a gravimeter reading error. DeepCausality Quantum plans the cheapest changes to an instrument that tell rival causes apart, prices each in instrument time, and reports the cause that remains.';

const seconds = (s: number) => s.toLocaleString('en-US');
const offset = Math.abs(GRAVIMETER.offsetUgal);
const leakPercent = Math.round(GRADIOMETER.leak * 100);
const e1 = gradiometerExperiments.find((e) => e.id === 'E1')!;

const run = `${GRAVIMETER.command}
${GRADIOMETER.command}
${verificationCommand('verification_v1_wavefront')}`;
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path="/sensing/">
  <SiteHeader slot="header" />

  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-inner">
      <p class="eyebrow">Quantum sensing</p>
      <h1>Discover the cause of a gravimeter reading error</h1>
      <div class="hero-grid">
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <p class="sub">
            A gravimeter reads {offset} µGal low, and seven known effects could each explain it. A
            longer passive run fits all seven equally well. DeepCausality Quantum plans the cheapest
            changes to the instrument that tell them apart, prices each change in instrument time,
            and runs them until one explanation remains.
          </p>
          <p class="gloss">
            The run below uses a published instrument and simulated observations. Eight further runs
            check the method against published results.
          </p>
          <div class="cta-row">
            <a class="btn btn-primary" href="#plan">See the plan <span class="btn-ico" aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
            <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="#run">Run it yourself</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-fig">
          <TrackFigure
            title="Seven explanations, one reading"
            rows={systematics.map((h) => ({ name: h.name, tag: h.id, value: GRAVIMETER.offsetUgal }))}
            min={-10}
            max={0}
            ticks={[-10, -5, 0]}
            hideValues
            marker={{ value: GRAVIMETER.offsetUgal, label: `passive reading, ${signed(GRAVIMETER.offsetUgal)} µGal` }}
          >
            Each candidate is sized to produce the offset, so all seven fit the passive reading
            equally well.
          </TrackFigure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <HomeSection id="why" ix="02 / 09" label="Why it matters" title="One effect carries most of the error.">
    <Fragment slot="lede">
      <p>
        An atom gravimeter measures gravity through the phase of falling atoms, and many effects
        shift that phase: the Earth’s rotation, stray magnetic fields, imperfect laser beams, a
        tilted instrument. An accuracy budget lists each effect with its size. The laser wavefront
        limits cold-atom gravimeters most (Karcher et al.), and in one published budget it carries
        {FIELD.budget.wavefrontUgal} µGal of a {FIELD.budget.totalUgal} µGal total uncertainty
        (Louchet-Chauvet et al.).
      </p>
      <p>
        The instruments now work outside the laboratory. On a {FIELD.vessel.vessel} vessel, a
        quantum gravimeter corrected the ship’s inertial navigation over
        {FIELD.vessel.routeNmi} nautical miles without GNSS. The corrected position ended
        {FIELD.vessel.aidedNmi} nautical miles from the truth, the uncorrected one
        {FIELD.vessel.unaidedNmi} (Everitt et al.). At sea, the ship’s rotation adds errors of its own
        (Lellouch and Holynski).
      </p>
      <p>
        Each new effect needs its own line in the budget before anyone can correct it, and each line
        rests on an experiment that moves that effect and leaves the others in place. Choosing those
        experiments is the library’s job.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <ul class="stats">
      <li class="stat panel corner-brackets">
        <p class="big num">{FIELD.budget.wavefrontUgal} <span>of</span> {FIELD.budget.totalUgal} µGal</p>
        <p class="what">The wavefront’s share of one gravimeter’s uncertainty budget.</p>
        <p class="src">{FIELD.budget.cite}, Table 1</p>
      </li>
      <li class="stat panel corner-brackets">
        <p class="big num">{FIELD.vessel.aidedNmi} <span>against</span> {FIELD.vessel.unaidedNmi} nmi</p>
        <p class="what">Position error after {FIELD.vessel.routeNmi} nautical miles at sea, with and without the quantum gravimeter.</p>
        <p class="src">{FIELD.vessel.cite}</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </HomeSection>

  <HomeSection id="plan" ix="03 / 09" label="Which change?" title="Skip the 5.6-hour scan and still find the wavefront.">
    <Fragment slot="lede">
      <p>
        Each effect answers a different change to the instrument. Turning the sensor head 180° flips
        the Coriolis and clipping shifts and leaves the rest in place. Reversing the laser’s wave
        vector flips the quadratic Zeeman shift. Halving the Rabi frequency halves the light shift.
        You write these responses once, in a response model, and the library computes what every
        change predicts under every candidate.
      </p>
      <p>
        Eight changes are on offer. The library prices each as its setup time plus the time its
        measurements take, and picks the cheapest set that separates every pair of candidates by at
        least {GRAVIMETER.floorBits} bits. That margin keeps the chance of mistaking one candidate
        for another below 1 in 64 (the Bhattacharyya bound; Kailath, 1967). Six changes do it, in
        {seconds(GRAVIMETER.planCost)} s.
      </p>
      <p>
        The plan skips the wavefront’s own test, a temperature scan with {seconds(20000)} s of setup.
        Once each of the other six candidates has a change that moves it, the wavefront is the
        explanation left when all six fail, so the plan finds it by elimination.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <SystematicsMatrix />
  </HomeSection>

  <HomeSection ix="04 / 09" label="The decision" title="Under three minutes for one cause.">
    <Fragment slot="lede">
      <p>
        Every run starts with the passive reading. The library drops any candidate the reading
        contradicts, plans for the rest, and runs the plan as a campaign. The campaign starts with
        the cheapest change that can separate the candidates still standing, compares every
        candidate’s prediction with the result, and stops as soon as one candidate fits and stands
        clear of the rest. When the quadratic Zeeman
        shift caused the offset, the first change settled it in {GRAVIMETER.cheapest} s. When the
        wavefront caused it, all six other candidates had to fail first, which took six changes and
        {seconds(GRAVIMETER.dearest)} s.
      </p>
      <p>
        The tide moves gravity by far more than {offset} µGal during a campaign; one published budget
        corrects −18.8 µGal for it (Louchet-Chauvet et al.). Each change therefore carries the tide at
        the time it runs, and the campaign plans again whenever the tide moves a prediction.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <CampaignBars />
    <div class="after">
      <EdgeCards show={['tilt', 'tide']} />
    </div>
  </HomeSection>

  <HomeSection ix="05 / 09" label="Missing cause" title="Leave out a cause, and another takes the blame.">
    <Fragment slot="lede">
      <p>
        The library weighs only the causes on your list, and its plan leaves out any change that
        only a missing cause would answer. If the missing cause answers the planned changes the way a
        listed cause does, the listed cause takes the blame, and the output reads like a correct run.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <div class="side">
      <EdgeCards show={['clipping']} />
    </div>
  </HomeSection>

  <HomeSection ix="06 / 09" label="Gradiometer" title="A one-minute test exposes a light leak.">
    <Fragment slot="lede">
      <p>
        A gravity gradiometer compares two atom clouds that share one laser, so their readings rise
        and fall together. If the shared laser alone causes that, the gradient stays correct. If
        light from one cloud’s detection leaks into the other’s, or the platform rotates, the
        gradient carries a bias. All four explanations fit the passive data.
      </p>
      <p>
        One 60 s change exposes a leak: light up one cloud, keep the other dark, and read the dark
        one. A leak lifts the dark cloud’s signal from {e1.predicts[1].toFixed(3)} to
        {e1.predicts[0].toFixed(3)}. A {leakPercent} % leak biases the gradient by
        {signed(GRADIOMETER.leakBias.eotvos)} E, or −3.6 × 10⁻⁷ s⁻². Finding rotation takes a
        deliberate turn of the platform, and the full plan takes {Math.round(GRADIOMETER.planCost)} s.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <TrackFigure
      title="B’s read-out, A lit, B dark"
      rows={gradiometerCandidates.map((c, k) => ({
        name: c.name,
        tag: c.id,
        value: e1.predicts[k],
        highlight: e1.predicts[k] !== e1.predicts[1],
      }))}
      min={0}
      max={0.1}
      ticks={[0, 0.05, 0.1]}
      digits={3}
      tickDigits={2}
    >
      The read-out each candidate predicts. Only a leak from A into B lifts it. Placeholders for a
      lab’s values: a {leakPercent} % leak and a {Math.round(GRADIOMETER.darkBackground * 100)} %
      dark background. <a href={`/examples/${GRADIOMETER.slug}/`}>The gradiometer example</a> shows
      the full run.
    </TrackFigure>
  </HomeSection>

  <HomeSection ix="07 / 09" label="Published results" title="Published numbers in, real answers out.">
    <Fragment slot="lede">
      <p>
        Simulated runs show the method works on its own terms. To test it against real data,
        {numberWord(VERIFICATION.count)} verifications feed published numbers into the same planner and judge,
        and check whether they reach the papers’ conclusions. All {VERIFICATION.checks} checks pass.
        Where a paper’s own numbers disagree with each other, the verification records it.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <div class="papers">
      <table class="table-stack">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Paper</th>
            <th scope="col">Question</th>
            <th scope="col">Same answer?</th>
            <th scope="col">Also found</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {verifications.map((v) => (
            <tr>
              <td data-label="Paper"><b>{v.source}</b><span class="ref">{v.ref}</span></td>
              <td data-label="Question">{v.question}</td>
              <td data-label="Same answer?" class="ans">{v.answer}</td>
              <td data-label="Also found">{v.also ?? '—'}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="more">
      <a href={`${CRATE_URL}/verification/sensing`}>The verifications’ README lists each check, its numbers and the command that reruns it <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
    </p>
  </HomeSection>

  <HomeSection ix="08 / 09" label="Limits" title="What your lab still supplies.">
    <ul class="limits">
      <li>The examples simulate their observations; the verifications read published values.</li>
      <li>A lab supplies the setup times, the tide, the leak and the dark background.</li>
      <li>A cause missing from the list stays unnamed until a change it contradicts runs.</li>
      <li>Measurement time follows white noise, within the instrument’s white-noise range.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="more"><a href="/boundaries/#sensing">Every limit, with the file that shows it <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></p>
  </HomeSection>

  <HomeSection id="run" ix="09 / 09" label="Run it" title="Verify the numbers.">
    <Fragment slot="lede">
      <p>
        From a clone of the repository, these commands rerun the gravimeter, the gradiometer and the
        wavefront verification. Both examples are seeded, so a second run prints the same output.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <Code code={run} lang="bash" themes={SHIKI} wrap />
  </HomeSection>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .hero { padding: var(--space-7) var(--space-4) var(--space-8); }
  @media (min-width: 720px) { .hero { padding: var(--space-8) var(--space-5) var(--space-9); } }
  .hero-inner { max-width: calc(var(--w-doc) - 2 * var(--space-5)); margin: 0 auto; }
  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.875rem, 3.2vw + 0.5rem, 3.25rem);
    line-height: var(--lh-display);
    letter-spacing: var(--ls-display);
    margin: var(--space-3) 0 var(--space-6);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .hero-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: var(--space-6); align-items: start; }
  @media (min-width: 1000px) {
    .hero-grid { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 5fr) minmax(0, 7fr); gap: var(--space-8); align-items: center; }
  }
  .sub { color: var(--fg-0); font-size: var(--t-h4); line-height: var(--lh-h4); max-width: var(--measure-lede); margin: 0 0 var(--space-5); }
  .gloss { color: var(--fg-1); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); max-width: var(--measure-lede); margin: 0 0 var(--space-5); }
  .cta-row { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--space-3); }
  .btn {
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: var(--space-2); height: 44px;
    padding: 0 var(--space-5); border-radius: var(--radius-sm); border: 1px solid transparent;
    font-size: var(--t-body-sm); font-weight: var(--fw-heading);
  }
  .btn-primary { background: var(--accent); color: var(--accent-ink); }
  .btn-ghost { color: var(--fg-0); border-color: var(--line-2); }

  .stats { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--space-4); }
  @media (min-width: 800px) { .stats { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; } }
  .stat { padding: var(--space-5); }
  .big { margin: 0 0 var(--space-2); font-size: var(--t-h3); color: var(--accent); }
  .big span { color: var(--fg-2); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); }
  .what { margin: 0 0 var(--space-2); color: var(--fg-0); }
  .src { margin: 0; color: var(--fg-2); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); }

  .papers table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; }
  .papers th, .papers td { text-align: left; vertical-align: top; padding: var(--space-3); border-top: 1px solid var(--line-1); }
  .papers thead th {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--t-eyebrow); letter-spacing: var(--ls-eyebrow);
    text-transform: uppercase; font-weight: 400; color: var(--fg-2); border-top: none;
  }
  .papers td { color: var(--fg-1); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); }
  .papers td b { display: block; color: var(--fg-0); font-weight: var(--fw-heading); }
  .papers .ref { color: var(--fg-2); font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--t-eyebrow); }
  .papers .ans { color: var(--accent); }
  @media (max-width: 719px) { .papers td { border-top: none; } }

  .after { margin-top: var(--space-5); }
  .side { max-width: var(--measure-lede); }

  .limits { margin: 0 0 var(--space-4); padding-left: var(--space-5); max-width: var(--measure); color: var(--fg-1); }
  .limits li { margin: var(--space-2) 0; }
  .more { margin: var(--space-5) 0 0; font-size: var(--t-body-sm); }
</style>
